1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,526
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,546 --> 00:00:07,199
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:07,599 --> 00:00:14,828
This is Lecture 15, pattern sewers, buttonholers, button sewers and bartackers.

4
00:00:15,228 --> 00:00:20,393
The electronic lockstitch machine from last time still needs the operator's

5
00:00:20,413 --> 00:00:22,052
hands to feed and steer.

6
00:00:22,072 --> 00:00:24,056
Today the program takes over.

7
00:00:24,076 --> 00:00:28,481
The fabric is clamped in a template and an X–Y table carries it.

8
00:00:28,881 --> 00:00:35,155
One rule runs through the whole lecture. While the needle is in the fabric,

9
00:00:35,175 --> 00:00:37,422
the template must not move.

10
00:00:38,942 --> 00:00:40,922
Here's the problem.

11
00:00:41,322 --> 00:00:48,770
A denim floor uses an electronic pattern sewer for the decorative stitching

12
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:50,363
on pocket flaps:

13
00:00:50,383 --> 00:00:54,943
about 60 stitches a flap, shaft at 2000 r/min,

14
00:00:54,963 --> 00:00:59,822
fabric plus lower template 1.5 millimetres thick.

15
00:01:00,222 --> 00:01:02,831
The customer wanted a bolder look,

16
00:01:02,851 --> 00:01:08,709
so the technician changed the stitch length from 3 to 6 millimetres and left

17
00:01:08,729 --> 00:01:09,946
the speed alone.

18
00:01:10,346 --> 00:01:11,726
Needles started breaking.

19
00:01:12,126 --> 00:01:14,619
Why does a longer stitch break needles?

20
00:01:15,019 --> 00:01:16,566
What speed should it run at?

21
00:01:16,966 --> 00:01:21,386
And how much does speed matter for output anyway?

22
00:01:21,786 --> 00:01:23,046
We'll answer at the end.

23
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:28,226
The pattern sewer: the template carries the fabric.

24
00:01:29,746 --> 00:01:31,410
Look at this exploded view.

25
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:38,010
Compared with a lockstitch machine, the head has no feed dog; the needle,

26
00:01:38,030 --> 00:01:42,185
take-up and hook mechanisms are exactly the same.

27
00:01:42,585 --> 00:01:49,265
At the end of the upper shaft is a direct-drive servo with an encoder,

28
00:01:49,285 --> 00:01:55,677
and the shaft angle it reads is the time base of the whole machine.

29
00:01:56,077 --> 00:02:02,070
The X–Y table takes over feeding: the Y beam moves front to back,

30
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:06,234
the X slide moves left and right on the beam,

31
00:02:06,254 --> 00:02:11,137
and the clamp frame and template ride on the X slide.

32
00:02:11,537 --> 00:02:15,486
A normal presser foot would block the template,

33
00:02:15,506 --> 00:02:20,637
so a small centre foot rides up and down with the needle bar.

34
00:02:22,157 --> 00:02:25,577
Needle in the fabric: the template stays still.

35
00:02:27,097 --> 00:02:33,647
On a lockstitch machine the feed dog only pushes once the needle has left the

36
00:02:33,667 --> 00:02:34,244
fabric;

37
00:02:34,264 --> 00:02:37,316
the eccentric's angle guarantees it.

38
00:02:37,716 --> 00:02:43,763
A pattern sewer has to obey the same rule in software: once the needle enters,

39
00:02:43,783 --> 00:02:45,862
the template must be still.

40
00:02:46,262 --> 00:02:48,222
Otherwise the hole is torn,

41
00:02:48,242 --> 00:02:52,842
and at worst the needle is bent into the hook point and breaks.

42
00:02:53,242 --> 00:02:58,746
Watch the close-up: the template stays still while the needle is in,

43
00:02:58,766 --> 00:03:01,102
and moves only once it's out.

44
00:03:01,502 --> 00:03:03,322
How big is the window?

45
00:03:03,722 --> 00:03:09,981
At top dead centre the point is 18 millimetres above the plate,

46
00:03:10,001 --> 00:03:15,563
so its height is 18 minus the needle-bar displacement x.

47
00:03:15,963 --> 00:03:23,124
It enters when that falls to the thickness t and leaves when it rises back.

48
00:03:24,644 --> 00:03:29,905
With the window known, how long does one stitch move take?

49
00:03:30,305 --> 00:03:35,706
Each axis has a maximum acceleration a and top speed v.

50
00:03:36,106 --> 00:03:40,733
Over a short distance it has to start braking before it reaches v,

51
00:03:40,753 --> 00:03:45,450
so the speed profile is a triangle whose area is the stitch length,

52
00:03:45,470 --> 00:03:48,971
and the time is twice the square root of s over a.

53
00:03:49,371 --> 00:03:55,978
In the example a is 50 metres per second squared and v is 1 metre per second,

54
00:03:55,998 --> 00:03:59,248
so v squared over a is 20 millimetres,

55
00:03:59,268 --> 00:04:03,896
and every pattern stitch falls in the triangle region.

56
00:04:04,296 --> 00:04:07,556
Acceleration is what decides.

57
00:04:07,956 --> 00:04:17,656
A 3-millimetre stitch takes 15.5 milliseconds, a 6-millimetre one 21.9.

58
00:04:19,176 --> 00:04:24,883
The time to turn through the window can't be shorter than the move,

59
00:04:24,903 --> 00:04:26,593
which rearranges to:

60
00:04:26,613 --> 00:04:31,636
speed limit equals the window W over 6 times the move time.

61
00:04:32,036 --> 00:04:44,929
At 1.5 millimetres thick, a 2-millimetre stitch allows 2680 r/min,

62
00:04:44,949 --> 00:04:53,734
3 millimetres 2188, 10 millimetres only 1198.

63
00:04:54,134 --> 00:05:00,994
Speed goes with one over root s: four times the stitch, half the speed.

64
00:05:01,394 --> 00:05:06,149
A 6-millimetre stack is about 16 percent slower.

65
00:05:06,549 --> 00:05:12,593
And a 45-degree stitch is about 19 percent faster,

66
00:05:12,613 --> 00:05:17,809
because each axis only moves s over root 2.

67
00:05:19,329 --> 00:05:24,589
This is the pattern-sewer simulation slowed down twenty times.

68
00:05:24,989 --> 00:05:35,089
On the left the stitches are 6 millimetres, on the right 3, both at 2000 r/min.

69
00:05:35,489 --> 00:05:42,173
In the dial, the blue arc is the window when the template may move,

70
00:05:42,193 --> 00:05:47,077
the grey arc is when the needle is in the fabric.

71
00:05:47,477 --> 00:05:53,218
Each dot is a needle point: blue means that move finishes inside the window,

72
00:05:53,238 --> 00:05:59,131
red means it doesn't, so the template is still moving when the needle goes in.

73
00:05:59,531 --> 00:06:03,551
Those red dots are the broken needles on the shop floor.

74
00:06:05,071 --> 00:06:07,731
Electronic cams and look-ahead.

75
00:06:09,251 --> 00:06:15,118
The shaft speed isn't constant: slow at the start, fast in the middle,

76
00:06:15,138 --> 00:06:19,071
slow before trimming, and it wobbles with load.

77
00:06:19,471 --> 00:06:23,109
If the X–Y moves were launched by time,

78
00:06:23,129 --> 00:06:27,423
any speed change would throw them out of step.

79
00:06:27,823 --> 00:06:32,205
So X and Y are written as functions of shaft angle:

80
00:06:32,225 --> 00:06:38,763
the shaft is the camshaft and the table stored in memory is its cam profile.

81
00:06:39,163 --> 00:06:40,823
That's an electronic cam.

82
00:06:41,223 --> 00:06:47,551
Every 250 microseconds the motion layer reads the encoder,

83
00:06:47,571 --> 00:06:52,039
looks up the table and sends the command.

84
00:06:52,439 --> 00:06:57,787
The drives lag by half to two milliseconds,

85
00:06:57,807 --> 00:07:04,030
and at 2800 r/min one millisecond is 16.8 degrees,

86
00:07:04,050 --> 00:07:08,899
so the lookup uses the predicted angle.

87
00:07:10,419 --> 00:07:15,078
This is really the cam chapter of mechanism design.

88
00:07:15,478 --> 00:07:18,901
Moving a distance s in a given time,

89
00:07:18,921 --> 00:07:25,023
the peak acceleration is always C times s over the time squared;

90
00:07:25,043 --> 00:07:26,458
only C changes.

91
00:07:26,858 --> 00:07:32,518
Constant acceleration, C equals 4, has the lowest peak and the highest speed,

92
00:07:32,538 --> 00:07:38,051
but the acceleration jumps at both ends, which rings the template and beam,

93
00:07:38,071 --> 00:07:43,805
and a belt-driven table is itself a spring, the last thing you want to excite.

94
00:07:44,205 --> 00:07:50,536
The modified trapezoid, 4.89, and the cycloid, 6.28,

95
00:07:50,556 --> 00:07:54,811
start and end at zero acceleration.

96
00:07:55,211 --> 00:07:56,889
With a cycloid,

97
00:07:56,909 --> 00:08:05,151
the move time grows 1.25 times and the speed limit is multiplied by 0.80.

98
00:08:05,551 --> 00:08:12,291
Hard laws for thin stock and short stitches; smooth ones for big templates.

99
00:08:13,811 --> 00:08:16,887
Stitch lengths vary within a pattern.

100
00:08:17,287 --> 00:08:21,267
Can each stitch just use its own limit?

101
00:08:21,667 --> 00:08:21,847
No.

102
00:08:22,247 --> 00:08:24,626
The shaft and hook have inertia,

103
00:08:24,646 --> 00:08:29,125
so the speed can change only by a limited amount per stitch.

104
00:08:29,525 --> 00:08:33,756
The controller looks a few stitches ahead and slows early,

105
00:08:33,776 --> 00:08:38,228
just like look-ahead in CNC: compute each stitch's own limit,

106
00:08:38,248 --> 00:08:42,846
sweep backwards so a slow stitch pulls the ones before it down,

107
00:08:42,866 --> 00:08:46,585
then sweep forwards so speed doesn't rise too fast.

108
00:08:46,985 --> 00:08:53,808
On the right the orange line is each stitch's limit and the blue line the

109
00:08:53,828 --> 00:08:55,026
actual speed,

110
00:08:55,046 --> 00:08:59,245
switching between fixed speed and look-ahead.

111
00:08:59,645 --> 00:09:02,505
Watch the blue line drop below the orange.

112
00:09:04,025 --> 00:09:05,005
Here's the example.

113
00:09:05,405 --> 00:09:12,691
With 2-millimetre stitches the machine runs at 2680 r/min;

114
00:09:12,711 --> 00:09:19,368
stitch 5 is 10 millimetres with a limit of only 1198,

115
00:09:19,388 --> 00:09:24,784
and the speed may change by 300 per stitch.

116
00:09:25,184 --> 00:09:35,618
After the backward pass the controller starts braking at stitch 1: 2398, 2098,

117
00:09:35,638 --> 00:09:45,804
1798, 1498, and exactly 1198 at stitch 5, then climbs back stitch by stitch.

118
00:09:46,204 --> 00:09:52,299
Without look-ahead, stitch 5 would try a 10-millimetre move at over 2600 r/min,

119
00:09:52,319 --> 00:09:53,848
far too little time,

120
00:09:53,868 --> 00:09:58,414
the template would still be moving when the needle entered,

121
00:09:58,434 --> 00:10:00,504
and the needle would break.

122
00:10:02,024 --> 00:10:06,065
Programming, templates and special machines.

123
00:10:07,585 --> 00:10:14,849
File formats differ between makers, but the content is much the same: a header,

124
00:10:14,869 --> 00:10:19,828
then a list of records, each a command plus ΔX and ΔY,

125
00:10:19,848 --> 00:10:22,963
the step from the previous stitch,

126
00:10:22,983 --> 00:10:27,205
stored as integers in units of 0.1 millimetre.

127
00:10:27,605 --> 00:10:33,697
Integer steps are compact and never accumulate rounding error.

128
00:10:34,097 --> 00:10:39,286
The programming software works in six steps: take the outline,

129
00:10:39,306 --> 00:10:45,757
split it into segments, divide them, add backtacks, order them, and simulate.

130
00:10:46,157 --> 00:10:50,275
Dividing uses n equals L over s rounded up,

131
00:10:50,295 --> 00:10:55,857
so every segment end gets a stitch and corners stay sharp.

132
00:10:56,257 --> 00:11:02,028
Between segments it trims, then jumps; the order decides how far it jumps.

133
00:11:03,548 --> 00:11:04,492
Bartackers,

134
00:11:04,512 --> 00:11:10,981
button sewers and buttonholers are the most numerous special machines in a

135
00:11:11,001 --> 00:11:12,208
garment plant.

136
00:11:12,608 --> 00:11:19,024
For control purposes they're small-field pattern sewers with a handful of

137
00:11:19,044 --> 00:11:21,933
fixed patterns where only length,

138
00:11:21,953 --> 00:11:24,578
width and stitch count change.

139
00:11:24,978 --> 00:11:30,289
On mechanical machines a new pattern means a new cam;

140
00:11:30,309 --> 00:11:35,017
electronic ones use two steppers and a program,

141
00:11:35,037 --> 00:11:38,940
an electronic cam in the literal sense.

142
00:11:39,340 --> 00:11:43,901
A bartack's stitches are generated from the parameters:

143
00:11:43,921 --> 00:11:48,400
a narrow base layer first, then a wide covering layer.

144
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:54,517
On a buttonholer the knife may cut only when the shaft has stopped at needle-up,

145
00:11:54,537 --> 00:11:58,247
and the machine can't start until the knife is back;

146
00:11:58,267 --> 00:12:01,260
both interlocks live in the state machine.

147
00:12:02,779 --> 00:12:07,284
These methods carry straight over to robots.

148
00:12:07,684 --> 00:12:12,999
A robot tracking a conveyor, gluing or sewing a moving panel,

149
00:12:13,019 --> 00:12:18,421
writes its position as a function of the belt encoder reading:

150
00:12:18,441 --> 00:12:24,544
an electronic cam, again with a predicted position to cover drive lag.

151
00:12:24,944 --> 00:12:31,280
An arm following a path with sharp corners has to slow down a few segments early,

152
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:33,164
exactly like look-ahead.

153
00:12:33,564 --> 00:12:39,536
Point-to-point moves use S-curves with continuous acceleration,

154
00:12:39,556 --> 00:12:45,624
for the same reason a pattern sewer picks a cycloid: no ringing.

155
00:12:46,024 --> 00:12:53,185
And joint motors are sized by peak torque first, then RMS torque,

156
00:12:53,205 --> 00:12:56,499
which is what decides heating.

157
00:12:58,019 --> 00:13:02,919
The everyday version of a pattern sewer is long-rope skipping.

158
00:13:03,319 --> 00:13:05,220
Two people turn the rope,

159
00:13:05,240 --> 00:13:11,443
and the jumper can run in or change places only while the rope is off the ground:

160
00:13:11,463 --> 00:13:13,671
that's the off-fabric window.

161
00:13:14,071 --> 00:13:19,444
The faster the rope turns, the shorter that window, just like a faster shaft.

162
00:13:19,844 --> 00:13:24,472
And the bigger the step you need, the less likely you'll make it,

163
00:13:24,492 --> 00:13:27,904
just like a longer stitch needing a longer move.

164
00:13:28,304 --> 00:13:33,387
When the rope spins fast, everyone takes small steps.

165
00:13:34,907 --> 00:13:37,327
Back to the opening question.

166
00:13:37,727 --> 00:13:48,638
A 3-millimetre stitch takes 15.5 milliseconds to move, a limit of 2188 r/min,

167
00:13:48,658 --> 00:13:51,051
so 2000 was fine.

168
00:13:51,451 --> 00:13:57,614
At 6 millimetres the move takes 21.9, the limit is only 1547,

169
00:13:57,634 --> 00:14:04,201
and at 2000 the template is still moving after the needle enters,

170
00:14:04,221 --> 00:14:06,330
which breaks needles.

171
00:14:06,730 --> 00:14:15,150
Slow down to about 1547 or below, or switch on automatic look-ahead.

172
00:14:15,550 --> 00:14:22,970
On 6-millimetre stock it could only reach 1297.

173
00:14:23,370 --> 00:14:31,511
And for output: 60 stitches sew in 1.8 seconds, while loading takes 8 to 10,

174
00:14:31,531 --> 00:14:35,592
so raising the speed buys very little.

175
00:14:37,112 --> 00:14:40,074
Now Virtual lab 15-1, the pattern-sewer simulation.

176
00:14:40,094 --> 00:14:42,881
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

177
00:14:42,901 --> 00:14:44,928
The results and tasks on the right,

178
00:14:44,948 --> 00:14:48,612
and the per-stitch speed chart at lower left, are blurred here,

179
00:14:48,632 --> 00:14:52,998
so you can read them on your own. On the left are the pocket-flap stitches,

180
00:14:53,018 --> 00:14:57,092
and in the dial the blue arc is the window when the template may move.

181
00:14:57,492 --> 00:15:06,452
Task one: at a fixed 2000 r/min, change the stitch from 3 to 6 millimetres.

182
00:15:06,472 --> 00:15:15,432
Some of the points turn red at once. Those are the moves that can't finish.

183
00:15:15,832 --> 00:15:20,646
Now drag the speed down, and the red stitches start to thin out.

184
00:15:20,666 --> 00:15:24,650
How far down until they're all blue is yours to find;

185
00:15:24,670 --> 00:15:27,143
then compare it with the formula.

186
00:15:27,543 --> 00:15:31,574
Task two: back to 3 millimetres and 2000 r/min,

187
00:15:31,594 --> 00:15:37,264
and raise the thickness from 1.5 to 6 millimetres. Watch the dial.

188
00:15:37,284 --> 00:15:40,023
The blue window keeps shrinking,

189
00:15:40,043 --> 00:15:44,333
and stitches that were all blue start turning red.

190
00:15:44,353 --> 00:15:47,523
Read the window angle in the results.

191
00:15:47,923 --> 00:15:53,423
Task three: switch to the star with 6-millimetre stitches,

192
00:15:53,443 --> 00:15:57,992
and raise the speed from 1200 r/min. Keep going,

193
00:15:58,012 --> 00:16:01,324
find which stitches turn red first,

194
00:16:01,344 --> 00:16:08,463
the horizontal and vertical ones or the diagonal ones, and think about why.

195
00:16:08,863 --> 00:16:12,184
Task four: switch to automatic look-ahead,

196
00:16:12,204 --> 00:16:16,560
try the three motion laws and compare the sewing times;

197
00:16:16,580 --> 00:16:21,971
then set the end margin to 15 degrees and see how much time it adds.

