207
00:17:05,643 --> 00:17:09,021
Your assignment has four questions. One:

208
00:17:09,041 --> 00:17:13,437
with fabric plus lower template 3 millimetres thick,

209
00:17:13,457 --> 00:17:19,468
use constant acceleration and a cycloid to find the speed limits for 2,

210
00:17:19,488 --> 00:17:22,526
4 and 8 millimetre stitches along X,

211
00:17:22,546 --> 00:17:26,348
then for a 4 millimetre stitch at 45 degrees,

212
00:17:26,368 --> 00:17:30,000
and explain why the axis components matter.

213
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:34,263
Two: the look-ahead example from the slides again,

214
00:17:34,283 --> 00:17:38,766
but now the speed may change by only 200 r/min per stitch.

215
00:17:38,786 --> 00:17:44,900
Find the speeds of stitches 1 to 8. How many stitches ahead must braking start?

216
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:49,673
Three is motor sizing, which this video skipped;

217
00:17:49,693 --> 00:17:54,981
it's in the slides and the textbook. For a 400 watt servo,

218
00:17:55,001 --> 00:18:00,015
check the peak and RMS torque on both the X and Y axes.

219
00:18:00,035 --> 00:18:04,225
Peak torque decides whether it can accelerate;

220
00:18:04,245 --> 00:18:10,174
RMS torque decides whether it overheats. For any axis that fails,

221
00:18:10,194 --> 00:18:16,214
give two ways to fix it. Four is in Lab 15-2, pattern programming:

222
00:18:16,234 --> 00:18:19,600
get the cycle time under 4.7 seconds.

223
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:21,392
Submit the report,

224
00:18:21,412 --> 00:18:26,253
the pattern file and the screenshots on the WenQuest platform.

225
00:18:27,773 --> 00:18:31,417
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

226
00:18:31,437 --> 00:18:36,912
with a 203.4-degree window and 50 metres per second squared per axis,

227
00:18:36,932 --> 00:18:41,532
what is the speed limit for a 4 millimetre stitch along X?

228
00:18:41,932 --> 00:18:47,981
Or, the shaft turns at 2400 r/min and the drive lags by 1.2 milliseconds.

229
00:18:48,001 --> 00:18:49,727
Without compensation,

230
00:18:49,747 --> 00:18:54,882
how many degrees of shaft angle does the template fall behind?

231
00:18:54,902 --> 00:18:58,041
Another asks about centre-foot timing.

232
00:18:58,441 --> 00:19:04,463
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

233
00:19:05,983 --> 00:19:07,520
Three things to remember.

234
00:19:07,920 --> 00:19:13,281
One: a pattern sewer replaces the feed dog with an X–Y table,

235
00:19:13,301 --> 00:19:17,779
and the shaft-encoder angle is the machine's clock.

236
00:19:18,179 --> 00:19:23,065
Two: the template must be still while the needle is in the fabric;

237
00:19:23,085 --> 00:19:27,451
the speed limit is the window over six times the move time,

238
00:19:27,471 --> 00:19:32,952
inversely proportional to root stitch length, and lower for thicker stock.

